18. Dispatch Decision
The engine returns a dispatch decision to the backend.
Conceptually:
DispatchDecision
│
├── requestId
├── selectedAmbulanceId
├── estimatedTravelTime
├── route
├── distance
├── score
└── generatedAt
Example:
Request: UK-0001

Selected:
AMB-02

ETA:
9 minutes

Distance:
3.5 km

Route:
N25 → N27 → N29 → N30
The backend then creates the assignment.
________________________________________
19. Assignment to Driver
The backend sends:
AMB-02
     ↓
Driver Application
The driver sees:
🚨 NEW EMERGENCY

Request: UK-0001
Emergency: Cardiac
Victims: 1

ETA: 9 minutes

[ ACCEPT ]
[ REJECT ]
The dispatch engine has now completed its initial selection phase.
________________________________________
20. Interaction with Cascading Fallback
The Intelligent Dispatch Engine should be designed so that it can be invoked repeatedly.
Example:
Initial ranking:

A2
A3
A1
A2 rejects.
The engine is called again:
Excluded:
A2

Remaining:
A3
A1
It recalculates:
A3 → Score 0.65
A1 → Score 0.78
Therefore:
A3 selected
If A3 also fails:
Exclude A3

Run matching again

A1 selected
This creates:
Request
 ↓
Match
 ↓
A2
 ↓
Reject
 ↓
Re-match
 ↓
A3
 ↓
Reject
 ↓
Re-match
 ↓
A1
The fallback mechanism is therefore not a separate matching algorithm.
It repeatedly invokes the same dispatch engine with updated candidate exclusions.
________________________________________
21. Avoiding Repeated Selection
The system must prevent the same failed ambulance from being immediately selected again.
Maintain:
Excluded Ambulances
Example:
Request UK-001

Excluded:
A2
A3
The next matching operation evaluates:
A1
A4
A5
rather than reconsidering A2/A3.
This state should be maintained in the backend/request attempt history.
________________________________________
22. Request Attempt History
Every dispatch attempt should be recorded.
Example:
Request: UK-001

Attempt 1
────────────
Ambulance: A2
Assigned: 10:00:02
Response: TIMEOUT

Attempt 2
────────────
Ambulance: A3
Assigned: 10:00:13
Response: REJECTED

Attempt 3
────────────
Ambulance: A1
Assigned: 10:00:25
Response: ACCEPTED
This information is extremely useful for research evaluation.
