37. Failure Handling
No available ambulance
Candidates = 0
       ↓
NO_AMBULANCE_AVAILABLE
No valid route
Ambulance exists
       ↓
Road network disconnected
       ↓
Candidate excluded
All candidates reject
A1 → Reject
A2 → Reject
A3 → Reject

No candidates remaining
       ↓
Dispatch failure state
Traffic changes
Current route becomes unavailable
       ↓
Recalculate route
________________________________________
38. Performance Considerations
The engine should avoid calculating routes for an unnecessarily huge fleet.
A practical simulation flow is:
All ambulances
       ↓
Availability filter
       ↓
Radius filter
       ↓
Top candidate set
       ↓
Route calculation
This reduces computational work.
For example:
1000 ambulances
       ↓
200 available
       ↓
50 within search radius
       ↓
Calculate routes for 50
The exact optimization can be evaluated as the simulation scale increases.
________________________________________
39. Future Scalability
For a larger system, the dispatch engine can eventually be extended with:
Geospatial indexing
       ↓
Fast candidate retrieval
       ↓
Distributed dispatch workers
       ↓
Caching
       ↓
Message queue
       ↓
Multiple dispatch regions
The current prototype does not need to implement a city-scale production infrastructure to demonstrate the algorithm.
The important objective is to establish the correct dispatch logic and measurable simulation first.
________________________________________
40. Simulation → Real Deployment
The most important architectural principle is to keep the dispatch algorithm independent of whether its input is simulated or real.
Current
Simulated Ambulance GPS
          ↓
Virtual Road Network
          ↓
Simulated Traffic
          ↓
Dispatch Engine
Future
Real Ambulance GPS
          ↓
Real Road Network
          ↓
Real-Time Traffic Data
          ↓
Dispatch Engine
The core decision-making layer can remain conceptually the same.
